---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureToSheet from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfSecureToSheet.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-to-excel">
  <PdfSecureToSheet client:load format="xlsx" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How table detection works here</h2>
      <p>
        A PDF stores no table. What it stores is text fragments at coordinates, and a table is
        simply the pattern where those fragments line up vertically. The extraction has three steps:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Lines. Every glyph run on a page is grouped by its baseline, so text at
          the same height becomes one row.
        </li>
        <li>
          Cells. Within a row, a horizontal gap more than about four space-widths
          wide is treated as a column separator rather than a word space. That single threshold is
          what turns "Invoice 4021 Acme Ltd 1,240.00" into three cells.
        </li>
        <li>
          Columns. The starting x position of every cell on the page goes into a
          histogram. Positions within the snapping tolerance of each other collapse into one column,
          and each cell is then assigned to the column it is nearest to. Columns that end up empty
          in every row are dropped.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        This is deliberately geometry-only. It does not look for ruled lines, because a great many
        real tables (bank statements, payroll runs, database report exports) have no ruling, and the
        ones that do are already well aligned.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The snapping slider is the control that matters</h2>
      <p>
        Everything else is automatic; this one number is worth understanding. It is the tolerance,
        in PostScript points (1/72 inch), for treating two cell start positions as the same column.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Symptom</th>
              <th scope="col">Fix</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>One logical column appears as two or three</td>
              <td>Raise the tolerance. The values do not start at identical x positions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Two columns are merging into one cell</td>
              <td>Lower the tolerance</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Right-aligned numbers scatter across columns</td>
              <td>
                Raise the tolerance. Right-aligned figures start at different x positions by
                definition, which is the hardest case for a start-position histogram
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Everything lands in one column</td>
              <td>
                The page probably has no table. The gaps between fields are ordinary word spaces
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The preview re-extracts as soon as you release the slider, so tuning it is a two-second
        loop.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>One worksheet per page</h2>
      <p>
        Each PDF page becomes its own sheet, named "Page 1", "Page 2" and so on. That is the right
        default for the most common source (a multi-page statement or report where each page has
        its own header row), and it keeps the pages separate if the column layout changes partway
        through the document.
      </p>
      <p>
        To stack them into one continuous table, open the workbook and copy the sheets together, or
        use <a href="/pdf-to-csv">PDF to CSV</a>, which writes every page into a single file with a
        blank line between them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Digitally generated PDFs only, and why</h2>
      <p>
        If a PDF was produced by scanning, its pages are photographs. There is no text to cluster,
        no coordinates to histogram and nothing this tool can do. It will tell you that plainly
        rather than returning an empty workbook.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reading a scan requires OCR, and we do not ship it. The engines small enough to run in a
        browser make character-level errors that are very bad in a spreadsheet. A misread digit in a
        column of figures is worse than no figures, because it looks correct. If your
        source is a scan, ask the issuer for the original export; banks, payroll systems and
        accounting packages nearly always have one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Check before you trust</h2>
      <p>
        Extraction from a document format that never intended to be extracted from is a heuristic,
        not a guarantee. Before using the numbers for anything that matters:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Compare the row count in the preview against the source page.</li>
        <li>Check the first and last row of each page, where the headers and totals are.</li>
        <li>
          Sum a column in Excel and compare it against the total printed in the PDF. If they match,
          the extraction is almost certainly clean.
        </li>
        <li>
          Watch for cells that ran together; they show as two values with a space between them.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The confidentiality point</h2>
      <p>
        The documents people convert with this kind of tool are bank statements, payroll runs,
        invoices, medical billing and internal reports. An upload puts a copy on infrastructure you do
        not control, kept for as long as that service's policy says. That is a promise, not a fact.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything on this page runs in your browser: pdf.js reads the text layer, the clustering is
        plain JavaScript, and the .xlsx is assembled and zipped in the same tab. Open the network
        panel while you convert, or switch off Wi-Fi first. Nothing changes.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
